==================================================
Lab 13: Visible Error-Based SQL Injection
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab is another form of blind SQL injection, but it turns out to be much faster and easier than the
previous two. The application uses a tracking cookie, just like the last two labs, and that value is used
inside a SQL query without being checked or cleaned. The difference here is that when something goes wrong with
the query, the application shows a very detailed, technical error message instead of a generic one. This
detailed error message accidentally reveals real data from the database, so we do not need to guess characters
one at a time at all.

Goal: use the database’s own verbose error messages to directly read out the administrator’s username and
password, then log in as administrator.

==============================
WHERE THE INJECTION POINT IS

Same as the last two labs, the vulnerable input is the TrackingId cookie. Its value goes straight into a SQL
query behind the scenes, so anything we put in it can change the structure of that query.

=============================
STEP 1: BREAK THE QUERY ON PURPOSE

We start by adding a single stray quote to the end of the cookie value. Since the cookie’s value is normally
wrapped in quotes inside the query, adding our own extra quote breaks the string open and causes a syntax error.

This time, because the application shows detailed errors, the response does not just say “something went
wrong,” it actually prints out the full SQL query it tried to run, including our cookie value sitting right
inside it, and explains clearly that there is an unclosed string literal. This tells us exactly where our input
lands inside the query, which is extremely useful information an attacker would not normally get to see.

=============================
STEP 2: FIX THE QUERY BACK TO VALID SQL

Now that we know where our input sits, we add two dashes right after our extra quote. This comments out
everything that comes after it, including the broken, unclosed quote that was causing the error. Sending this
version causes no error at all, confirming the query is syntactically valid again, and that we are now fully in
control of how the query ends.

=============================
STEP 3: INTRODUCE A TYPE-MISMATCH ON PURPOSE

Next, we build a small subquery that selects a harmless value, and wrap it with a function that forces the
database to convert that value into a whole number data type. Since our subquery initially just selects the
number 1, converting it to a number causes no problem, so no error appears.

To make this condition logically connect back to the main query, we compare the number 1 against the result of
our subquery using an equals sign, so the overall condition reads as a normal true or false comparison instead
of a standalone subquery, which the database requires in this context.

=============================
STEP 4: REPLACE THE HARMLESS VALUE WITH REAL DATA

Now, instead of selecting a harmless number, we change the subquery to select the username column from the
users table instead. A username is text, not a number, so trying to force it into a numeric type is now
genuinely impossible, and the database throws an error again.

At this point, something else happens: the cookie value becomes too long, since we are adding more and more
text to it, and the website trims off part of it due to a character limit. This cuts off our comment dashes at
the end, which breaks the query again in an unintended way.

=============================
STEP 5: FREE UP SPACE IN THE COOKIE

To fix the character limit problem, we simply delete the original tracking ID value entirely and replace it with
only our injected payload. The original value was never actually needed for the injection to work, it was just
part of the cookie by default, so removing it frees up enough space for our full payload, comment dashes
included, to fit without being cut off.

=============================
STEP 6: HANDLE MULTIPLE ROWS

Sending the corrected, shorter payload produces a new kind of error, one generated by the database itself rather
than a generic syntax complaint. This error explains that the subquery unexpectedly returned more than one row.
This actually confirms something important: the query is running successfully against real data, it is just
that selecting the username column without any row limit pulls back every username in the table at once, which
breaks the single-value comparison we are trying to make.

We fix this by adding a row limit to the subquery, so it only returns exactly one row, keeping the comparison
valid.

=============================
STEP 7: READ THE LEAKED DATA DIRECTLY FROM THE ERROR

With the row limit in place, the query runs successfully up until the actual type conversion step, where it
tries to turn the real username text into a number and fails. Crucially, the error message generated by this
specific kind of failure includes the exact text value it was trying to convert, directly inside the error
description. This means the real username appears in plain text, right there in the error message on the page.

Once we see the first username this way, we simply change the subquery to select the password column instead,
keeping everything else the same, including the row limit. This causes the exact same kind of conversion error,
except this time the value printed inside the error message is the real password.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and find a request containing the TrackingId cookie using Burp Suite.
Add a single quote to the cookie value, and confirm a detailed error appears showing the broken query.
Add two dashes after the quote, and confirm the error disappears, meaning the query is valid again.
Add a subquery selecting a harmless number, cast to an integer type, and confirm it still works without error.
Change the subquery to select the username column from the users table instead, and observe an error, along
with the cookie being cut off due to a character limit.
Delete the original tracking ID value to free up space, keeping only the injected payload.
Add a row limit to the subquery to avoid a “more than one row” error.
Send the request and read the leaked username directly from the error message text.
Change the subquery to select the password column instead, keeping the row limit, and read the leaked password
the same way.
Log in manually as administrator using the leaked password to solve the lab.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, for sending web requests, and the re library, for searching text using
patterns.

It asks for the base address of the lab, and removes any trailing slash.

A session is created, and a single request is sent to the home page just to receive a genuine TrackingId cookie
from the server. This value is saved, mainly just to confirm the server is responding normally, since the actual
attack payload does not reuse this original value at all.

A helper function called send_payload is defined. It takes a complete SQL injection string, builds a cookie
using just that string, with no original tracking ID attached, matching the manual steps where the original
value was deleted to free up space. It sends this as a request and returns the full response object, so the
rest of the script can inspect both the status code and the raw page text.

The script first sends a simple single quote by itself, just to confirm this still causes an error, matching
step one of the manual walkthrough, mainly as a sanity check before moving further.

It then sends the real payload for leaking a username: a quote, AND, the number 1, an equals sign, a CAST
function wrapping a subquery that selects the username column from the users table with a row limit of one as
an integer, and two dashes at the end. This exactly matches the final working payload built step by step in the
walkthrough.

The response text is searched using a regular expression looking for the specific error wording the database
produces when a type conversion fails, capturing whatever text sits inside the quotation marks in that error
message. This captured text is the actual leaked username.

If this pattern is not found at all, the script prints the raw beginning of the response text, so the real
wording of the error can be inspected and the pattern adjusted if needed, rather than failing silently.

Once the username is found and printed, the exact same process repeats, except the subquery is changed to select
the password column instead of username, keeping the row limit the same. The same regular expression is used to
pull the leaked password out of the new error message.

Finally, both the leaked username and password are printed together, along with a reminder to log in manually
using these credentials on the login page.

=============================
WHY THIS TECHNIQUE NEEDS FAR FEWER REQUESTS

In the previous two labs, blind injection required asking the database one true or false question per guess,
repeated many times per character, and once per character across the entire password. This lab instead tricks
the database into directly printing the real value inside its own error message, as a side effect of a failed
type conversion. This means a single well-crafted request can leak an entire piece of data at once, rather than
needing dozens or hundreds of requests just to extract one password.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is still the same root cause as every earlier lab, a cookie value is joined directly into a
SQL query without being checked or cleaned. The new factor here is that the application also displays extremely
detailed, technical database error messages directly on the page, rather than hiding them behind a generic
message. These two problems combined are what make this attack so much faster than the earlier blind techniques.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that cookie values, like any other user input, can never change the structure of a
SQL query, regardless of what type-mismatch tricks an attacker might attempt.

Other good practices:

Never show raw, detailed database error messages to users in a live application. Error messages meant for
developers during testing should be logged privately on the server, while users should only ever see a generic,
non-technical error page.
Regularly review what information error pages might be leaking, since even something that seems like just a
technical inconvenience, such as a type conversion failure, can become a serious data leak if the error message
happens to include the actual value involved.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A detailed, verbose error message can leak real data directly, without needing to guess anything character by
character.
Forcing a type conversion to fail, such as trying to turn text into a number, is a common way to trigger this
kind of data-leaking error.
Character limits on input fields like cookies can interfere with longer injection payloads, and removing
unnecessary parts of the original value can free up the space needed.
Always limiting a subquery to return only one row is important whenever a single value comparison is being
built, otherwise the database may reject the query for returning too many rows instead of giving the error we
actually want.
Hiding detailed error messages from end users is one of the simplest and most effective ways to prevent this
specific type of data leak, even if the underlying SQL injection vulnerability still technically exists.
